[Simplification] With this change, the `context == null` branch makes the same scoped delegate call as the normal path. Only the step before that call differs: logging or ending the span. You could branch only on that step and keep a single scoped call afterward. This would remove the duplicated `try` block and the early `return`:

```java
if (context == null) {
  // this is unexpected
  logger.fine("context was never set");
} else {
  instrumenter().end(context, otelRequest, getResponseFromHttpContext(), null);
}

try (Scope ignored = parentContext.makeCurrent()) {
  completeDelegate(result);
}
```

The same shape would work in `failed` and `cancelled`, and in the matching 5.0 `WrappedFutureCallback`. It also makes clear that ending the span is conditional, while calling the delegate under the caller context always happens. Ordering and scope cleanup stay the same.

